<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagrati Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1003</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bagrati Cathedral is an eleventh-century cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, built under Bagrat III, the first king of united Georgia, and completed in the early 1000s. It was a masterpiece of medieval Georgian architecture until an explosion during the Ottoman invasion of 1692 ruined it. Controversially restored in 2012, it is a symbol of Georgian unity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagrati-cathedral.jpg" alt="Bagrati Cathedral" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin Konsek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014_Kutaisi,_Katedra_Bagrati_(19).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cathedral of the Dormition, known as the Kutaisi Cathedral and more often as Bagrati Cathedral, stands on Ukimerioni Hill in the centre of Kutaisi, in the Imereti region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. It was built in the early years of the eleventh century during the reign of King Bagrat III, from whom it takes its name, meaning Bagrat's cathedral. An inscription on the north wall states that the floor was laid in the year 223 of the chronicon, that is, 1003.</p><p>The cathedral is dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assumption-of-mary">Dormition of the Mother of God</a>, and its main feast day is Mariamoba. It is described as a masterpiece of medieval Georgian architecture, and William Lethaby called it the finest of Georgian monuments. King George I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> is buried there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1692 the cathedral was devastated during the Ottoman invasion of the Kingdom of Imereti. The cupola and the ceiling collapsed, and it suffered heavy damage over the following centuries. Conservation, restoration and archaeological work began in the 1950s under the Georgian architect Vakhtang Tsintsadze. His restoration was carried out in six stages and continued through 1994. In that year the cathedral was included in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage list together with the Gelati Monastery as a single entity. In 2001 ownership passed from the Georgian state to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>, and it is the cathedral seat of the Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, with a monastery on the grounds and regular services.</p><p>Further works to reconstruct the building ended in 2012 and were controversial. In 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> placed the cathedral on its list of World Heritage in Danger, fearing for the structural integrity and authenticity of the site, and in 2017 it removed Bagrati Cathedral from the World Heritage list, while Gelati Monastery kept its status. The cathedral remains a symbol of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> and one of its main tourist attractions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bagrati Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rock-cut monastery of Vardzia, consecrated in the 1180s, the <b>Bagrati Cathedral</b> at Kutaisi and the rebuilt Svetitskhoveli Cathedral at Mtskheta, finished around 1029, show the ambition of Georgian church architecture, while goldsmiths and fresco painters produced works of high quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The church of Gelati and the remains of the <b>Bagrati Cathedral</b>, damaged by an Ottoman explosion in 1692, are today among Georgia’s most important monuments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
